          body: "The side of a balance follows directly from the double entry rules. Ask one question about each account: what kind of account is it? Assets, expenses, purchases and drawings are increased by debits, so they carry debit balances. Capital, liabilities, income and revenue are increased by credits, so they carry credit balances.\n\nReturns are the opposite of the account they reverse. Sales returns reduce revenue, so they have a debit balance. Purchases returns reduce purchases, so they have a credit balance. Discounts follow the same logic: discount allowed is an expense (debit) and discount received is income (credit).\n\nTwo items catch students out. A bank overdraft is money owed to the bank, so it is a liability with a credit balance even though it sits in the cash book. Only the opening inventory appears in the trial balance, because the closing inventory has not yet been entered in the ledger when the trial balance is drawn up.",
