          body: "Some errors break the equality of debits and credits, and the trial balance will catch them. The most common is a one-sided entry: an amount is entered in the cash book but never posted to the ledger account, or it is posted twice to one account. Another is posting to the wrong side, for example putting discount allowed on the credit side of its account. A wrong-side error creates a difference of twice the amount, because the debit column is short by the amount and the credit column is too large by the same amount.\n\nAddition errors also show up. If the sales journal is overcast, the sales account receives a credit that is too large while the individual customer accounts are correct. If a ledger account is added wrongly, its balance is wrong on one side only. Finally, a balance copied into the wrong column of the trial balance, or left out of the trial balance altogether, makes the totals disagree even though the ledger itself is correct.",
