      mcq: [
        { id: "mcq-3.1-1", question: "What is the main purpose of preparing a trial balance?", choices: ["To check the arithmetical accuracy of the double entry", "To calculate the profit for the year", "To show the financial position of the business", "To prove that no errors have been made in the ledger"], explanation: "A trial balance tests that total debits equal total credits. Profit and financial position come from the financial statements, and an agreed trial balance does not prove the ledger is free of errors." },
        { id: "mcq-3.1-2", question: "Which balance would appear in the credit column of a trial balance?", choices: ["Rent received", "Drawings", "Carriage inwards", "Sales returns"], explanation: "Rent received is income, so it has a credit balance. Drawings, carriage inwards and sales returns all have debit balances." },
        { id: "mcq-3.1-3", question: "Which error would be revealed by a trial balance?", choices: ["Posting only the debit entry of a credit purchase", "Omitting a credit sale from the books completely", "Debiting a new machine to the repairs account", "Overcasting the wages account and the sales account by the same amount"], explanation: "A one-sided posting makes the debit total larger than the credit total. The other three are errors of omission, principle and compensating errors, which leave the totals equal." },
        { id: "mcq-3.1-4", question: "A delivery van bought for $8,000 was debited to the purchases account. What type of error is this?", choices: ["Error of principle", "Error of commission", "Error of original entry", "Complete reversal of entries"], explanation: "A non-current asset was treated as an expense, so the wrong class of account was used. That is an error of principle, not commission (which would be the wrong account of the same class)." },
        { id: "mcq-3.1-5", question: "A credit sale of $240 to A. Fouad was debited to the account of A. Fawzy, another customer. What type of error is this?", choices: ["Error of commission", "Error of principle", "Error of omission", "Compensating error"], explanation: "The correct amount and side were used but in the wrong account of the same class (both are trade receivables). This is an error of commission." },
        { id: "mcq-3.1-6", question: "An invoice for $630 was entered in the purchases journal as $360 and posted to both accounts at that figure. What type of error is this?", choices: ["Error of original entry", "Error of commission", "Compensating error", "Error of principle"], explanation: "The wrong amount was used in the book of prime entry, so both the debit and the credit carry the same wrong figure. The trial balance still agrees." },
        { id: "mcq-3.1-7", question: "The debit column of a trial balance totals $54,320 and the credit column totals $54,020. What suspense account entry is needed?", choices: ["Credit balance of $300", "Debit balance of $300", "Credit balance of $600", "Debit balance of $150"], explanation: "The difference is $300 and the credit column is the smaller side, so the suspense account has a credit balance of $300 to make the totals agree." },
        { id: "mcq-3.1-8", question: "The purchases account was overcast by $250 and the discount received account was also overcast by $250. What type of errors are these?", choices: ["Compensating errors", "Errors of original entry", "Errors of principle", "Complete reversal of entries"], explanation: "An extra $250 debit in purchases is cancelled by an extra $250 credit in discount received, so the trial balance agrees. Two errors that cancel each other are compensating errors." },
        { id: "mcq-3.1-9", question: "A payment of $500 to a supplier was debited to the bank account and credited to the supplier's account. What type of error is this?", choices: ["Complete reversal of entries", "Error of commission", "Error of omission", "Error of principle"], explanation: "The correct accounts and amount were used but the debit and credit were swapped. This is a complete reversal of entries, and the trial balance still agrees." },
        { id: "mcq-3.1-10", question: "A trainee's trial balance has a debit total of $62,500 and a credit total of $58,900. The debit column wrongly includes closing inventory of $4,000, and discount allowed of $200 has been entered in the credit column. What are the corrected totals?", choices: ["$58,700 in each column", "Debit $58,500 and credit $58,700", "Debit $62,700 and credit $58,700", "Debit $58,300 and credit $59,100"], explanation: "Remove the closing inventory: 62,500 minus 4,000 = 58,500. Move discount allowed (an expense) to the debit column: debit 58,500 plus 200 = 58,700 and credit 58,900 minus 200 = 58,700. The distractors forget to add the $200 to the debit column, leave the inventory in, or move the discount the wrong way." },
      ],
