          heading: "Statement of corrected profit and the statement of financial position",
          body: "A statement of corrected profit starts with the draft profit for the year. Underneath, list each error with the amount by which the correction increases or decreases profit, and write no effect where that is the case. Add the increases, deduct the decreases and show the corrected profit for the year. Keep the layout clear: one line per error, numbered as in the question.\n\nErrors also affect the statement of financial position. A non-current asset wrongly debited to an expense means non-current assets were understated. A missing credit to a customer account means trade receivables were overstated. A payment not posted to a supplier means trade payables were overstated. Because profit flows into capital, every error that changes profit also changes closing capital by the same amount. Drawings posted to an expense account understate drawings and understate profit by the same figure, so closing capital is unchanged even though two lines were wrong.\n\nIf a suspense balance remains after the known corrections, show it in the statement of financial position: a debit balance as a current asset, a credit balance as a current liability. This should be temporary.",
